<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Norway (1814)</h1><p class="st-meta">1814 CE – 1814 CE · capital Christiania</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The brief independent Norway of 1814, which adopted the Eidsvoll constitution, still in force, before entering union with Sweden.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kiel">Treaty of Kiel</a> in January 1814 transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, the Danish prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-viii-of-denmark">Christian Frederik</a>, governor in Norway, led resistance. A constituent assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidsvoll">Eidsvoll</a> adopted a liberal constitution on 17 May 1814 and elected him king.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The constitution, inspired by the American and French revolutions, divided power between king, an elected parliament, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a>, and independent courts, and gave the vote to a relatively broad group of men. The new state had little time to govern: the great powers backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden's</a> claim, and the country was blockaded.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> invaded in July 1814, and after a short war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-moss">Convention of Moss</a> on 14 August preserved the constitution in return for union. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-viii-of-denmark">Christian Frederik</a> abdicated in October, and on 4 November the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a> elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xiii-of-sweden">Charles XIII</a> king, beginning <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sweden-norway">Sweden-Norway</a>. 17 May remains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway's</a> national day.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-norway-1814.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Norway (1814)" width="320" height="233"><figcaption>Image: Original: Fredrik Meltzer Vector: Gutten på Hemsen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Norway.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1814 CE – 1814 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Christiania</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Christian Frederik</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Norwegian (Danish written standard)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Independent Norway 1814, Eidsvoll Norway</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1814&amp;focus=kingdom-of-norway-1814" aria-label="Kingdom of Norway (1814) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4904 169 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Norway (1814) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5224 425L5274 535M5224 425L5381 440"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5274 535h0M5381 440h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 425h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1814&amp;focus=kingdom-of-norway-1814">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
